{"v":2,"lessons":[{"id":"es:c0","title":"@c_pron","level":"@beginner","kind":"grammar","intro":"The vowels in Spanish are short crisp sounds. They are not dragged out like some English vowels. Spanish makes no distinction between \"long\" and \"short\" vowels and Spanish-speakers are unlikely to even hear a difference. There is however a distinction between stressed and unstressed.","sections":[{"heading":"Vowels","body":"a : like 'a' in \"father\" · e : between 'ay' in \"pay\" and 'e' in \"pet\" · i : like 'ee' in \"see\" · o : like 'o' in \"roll\" · u : like 'u' in \"rule\" · y : like 'ee' in \"see\". Very rarely used at the middle or ending of words. Like \"y\" in young at the beginning of words."},{"heading":"Consonants","body":"b : like 'b' in \"elbow\" at the beginning of a word and after 'm': boca (mouth). A soft vibration sound almost like English 'v' elsewhere, specifically a 'v' sound but with your two lips pressed together in a 'b' or 'p' shape with the airflow of a 'v'. · c : In most cases it is pronounced like 'c' in \"scare\": calle (street). When followed by 'e' or 'i', it is like 's' in \"supper\" (in the Americas, the Canaries and some parts of the Philippines) or 'th' in \"thin\" (Spain): cine (cinema, pronounced by Latin Americans as SEE-nay, Spaniards as THEE-nay) · ch : like 'ch' in \"touch\": muchacho (boy) [tʃ] · d : like 'd' in \"dog\" at the beginning of a sentence or after 'n' or 'l'; like the 'th' in \"the\" between vowels: de (of/from), pasado (the adjective or noun past). You're usually fine just using the 'th' sound as in \"the\". · f : like 'f' in \"fine\": faro (lighthouse) · g : when followed by 'e' or 'i', like a throaty 'h' (general = heh-neh-RAHL, meaning general), otherwise like 'g' in \"ago\" (gato, cat). In the clusters \"gue\" and \"gui\", the 'u' serves only to change the sound of the consonant and is silent (guitarra, guitar), unless it bears a diaeresis, as in \"güe\" and \"güi\" (pedigüeño, beggar). In between vowels, it tends to be weakened to a softer sound, [ɣ]. · h : silent: hora= OR-ah (hour). Pronounced like a softer 'j' only in foreign words. · j : like a throaty 'h' in \"ha\": jamón (ham), the sound of Scottish or German \"ch\" as in \"Loch\" is close [x] · k : like 'k' in \"ski\" or silent like Spanish H: kilo knox The letter K is only used in foreign words (kárate, kilo, Kevin, Karen, etc.). · l : like 'l' in \"love\": lápiz (pen) · ll : Varies substantially by dialect. The easiest widely understandable pronunciation is like 'y' in \"year\": llamar (to call). Pronounced like a 'zh' [ʒ] as in 'Zhivago' or 'sh' [ʃ] as in 'show' only in Argentina and Uruguay; in some other countries it may be pronounced like an English 'j' [dʒ] as in \"ninja\" or like 'ly' [ʎ] as in the English word \"million\". The [ʎ] pronunciation was once enforced in school education as the \"only correct\" form especially in Francoist Spain but is now retreating northwards in Spain and otherwise only prevalent among bilingual speakers whose other language (e.g. Catalan or Quechua) contains this phoneme. · m : like 'm' in \"mother\": mano (hand, a rare example of a word whose grammatical gender breaks the rules outlined above—\"la mano\" is correct) · n : like 'n' in \"nice\", and like 'n' in \"anchor\": noche (night), ancla (anchor) · ñ : like 'ny' in \"canyon\": cañón (canyon) [ɳ], piñata. This is a separate letter in the Spanish alphabet. Pronouncing this as \"n\" will usually be intelligible but can sometimes make an entirely separate word. For instance, año is \"year\" but ano is \"anus\"—you'll want to avoid saying that you have 33 anuses when you mean to say that you're 33 years old. · p : like 'p' in \"spit\": perro (dog) · q : like 'k' in \"ski\" (always with a silent \"u\"): queso, pronounced KAY-so (cheese) · r, rr : Spanish has two 'r' sounds, both of which are different from their counterpart in English. Some effort should be made to approximate each of them, to help listeners distinguish between perro (\"dog\") and pero (\"but\") — or perhaps to understand you at all: · s : like 's' in \"son\": sopa (soup); in Spain, it is often pronounced like a soft, palatised \"sh\" at the end of a word or syllable. · t : like 't' in \"stop\": tapa (top) · v : Pronounced the same way as b (see above): like 'b' in \"elbow\" at the beginning of a word and after 'n'; closer to the English 'v' sound elsewhere. To distinguish v from b when saying the name of the letter, one says \"ve chica\" or \"be grande\" to indicate which; native Spanish speakers may not hear the difference between \"vee\" and \"bee\". · w : like 'w' in \"weight\" in English words, whisky, (pronounced \"WEESS-kee\"). Like 'b' in \"bed\" in German words. This letter is never used in native Spanish words and you can live your entire life in a Hispanic country and never hear it outside of the word \"whisky\". · x : like 'x' in \"flexible\" (flexible). Like 'ss' in \"hiss\" at beginning of a word (xilófono, xylophone). Like a throaty 'h' in the words México, mexicano, Oaxaca, and oaxaqueño. Often found in words of indigenous American origin where it may not follow Spanish pronunciation logic. · y : like 'y' in \"yes\": payaso (clown). Like 'y' in \"boy\": hoy (now). Pronounced like a 'zh' [ʒ] as in 'Zhivago' or 'sh' [ʃ] as in 'show' only in Argentina and Uruguay: yo no sé (I don't know), pronounced \"zhoh noh say\". · z : like 's' in \"supper\" (Latin America, parts of Spain), like 'th' in \"thin\" (most of Spain) [θ]: zorro (fox). See c above."},{"heading":"Diphthongs","body":"ai, ay : like 'eye': baile (BAI-leh, dance) · au : like 'ow' in \"cow\": causa (KOW-sah, cause) · ea : like 'eh-ah': fea (FEH-ah, ugly) · ei, ey : like 'ay' in \"say\": reina, rey. (RAY-nah, queen) · eu : like 'eh-oo': euro (\"EH-oo-roh\") · ia : like 'ee-yah': piano (pee-YAH-noh) · ie : like 'ee-yeh': pie (PYEH, foot) · io : like 'ee-oh': dio (DYOH, gave) · iu : like 'ee-oo': ciudad (see-oo-DAHD, city) · oi, oy : like 'oy' in \"boy\": soy (soy, I am) · ua : like 'wa' in \"wallet\": cuatro (KWAH-troh, four) · ue : like 'we' in \"well\": puedo (PWEH-doh, I can) · ui, uy : like 'wee' in \"ween\": ruido (RRWEE-doh, noise) · uo : like \"wo\" in \"won't\": averiguo (ah-beh-REE-gwoh, I find out)"}],"phrases":[]},{"id":"es:c2","title":"@c_first","level":"@beginner","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"Cerrado","f":"CLOSED","r":"sehr-RAH-doh"},{"t":"Entrada","f":"ENTRANCE","r":"ehn-TRAH-dah"},{"t":"Salida","f":"EXIT","r":"sah-LEE-dah"},{"t":"Empuje/Empujar","f":"PUSH","r":"ehm-POO-heh/ehm-poo-HAHR"},{"t":"Tire/Tirar/Jale","f":"PULL","r":"TEE-reh/tee-RAHR/HAH-leh"},{"t":"Baños / Servicios / Aseos; also S.H. or S.S.H.H. for Servicios Higiénicos","f":"TOILET","r":"bahn-YOS"},{"t":"Hombres / Caballeros","f":"MEN","r":"OHM-brehss"},{"t":"Mujeres / Damas / Señoras","f":"WOMEN","r":"moo-HEH-rehss"},{"t":"No fumar/fume","f":"DON'T SMOKE","r":"noh foo-MAHR/FOO-meh"},{"t":"Prohibido }}","f":"FORBIDDEN","r":"pro-ee-BEE-doh"},{"t":"Hola","f":"Hello/Hi (informal)","r":"OH-lah"},{"t":"Que pase/pases un buen día","f":"Have a good day","r":"keh PAH-seh/PAH-sehs un BWEHN DEE-ah"},{"t":"¿Cómo estás?","f":"How are you? (informal)","r":"KOH-moh ehs-TAHSS?"},{"t":"¿Qué tal?","f":"How are you? (informal)","r":"keh TAHL"},{"t":"¿Cómo está usted?","f":"How are you? (formal)","r":"KOH-moh ehs-TAH oos-TEHDH?"},{"t":"Muy bien, gracias.","f":"Fine, thank you","r":"MOO-ee bee-YEHN, GRAH-see-YAHSS"},{"t":"¿Cómo te llamas?","f":"What is your name? (informal)","r":"KOH-moh TAY YAH-mahs?"},{"t":"¿Cómo se llama usted?","f":"What is your name? (formal)","r":"KOH-moh SAY YAH-mah oos-TEHD?"},{"t":"¿Quién eres?","f":"Who are you? (informal)","r":"KYEN EH-rehs?"},{"t":"¿Quién es usted?","f":"Who are you? (formal)","r":"KYEN ehs oos-TEHD?"},{"t":"Me llamo ______","f":"My name is ______","r":"MEH YAH-moh _____"},{"t":"Encantado/a","f":"Nice to meet you","r":"ehn-kahn-TAH-doh''/''ehn-kahn-TAH-dah"},{"t":"Mucho gusto.","f":"It's a pleasure to meet you","r":"MOO-choh GOOS-toh"},{"t":"Me gustaría presentarle a....","f":"I'd like to introduce you to....","r":"meh goos-tah-REE-ah preh-sehn-TAHR-leh ah"},{"t":"¿Cuántos años tiene usted?","f":"How old are you? (formal)","r":"KWAHN-tohs AH-nyohs TYEH-neh oos-TEHD"},{"t":"Tengo....años.","f":"I'm....years old.","r":"TEHN-goh....AH-nyohs"},{"t":"Por favor or just \"porfa\"","f":"Please","r":"POHR fah-BOHR"},{"t":"Gracias","f":"Thank you","r":"GRAH-syahs"},{"t":"De nada","f":"You're welcome","r":"DEH NAH-dah"},{"t":"Sí","f":"Yes","r":"SEE"},{"t":"No","f":"No","r":"NOH"},{"t":"Disculpe","f":"Excuse me (getting attention)","r":"dees-KOOL-peh"},{"t":"Perdone","f":"Excuse me (begging pardon)","r":"pehr-DOHN-eh"},{"t":"Permiso","f":"Excuse me (may I get by?)","r":"pehr-MEE-so"},{"t":"Lo siento","f":"I'm sorry","r":"LOH SYEHN-toh"},{"t":"Adiós / Hasta luego","f":"Goodbye","r":"ah-DYOHS"},{"t":"Hablo un poco español.","f":"I speak a little Spanish.","r":"ah-BLOH oon POH-koh ehs-pah-NYOHL"},{"t":"No hablo español","f":"I can't speak Spanish (well)","r":"noh AH-bloh (byehn) ehs-pah-NYOL"},{"t":"¿Hablas inglés?","f":"Do you speak English? (informal)","r":"AH-blahs een-GLEHS?"},{"t":"¿Habla usted inglés?","f":"Do you speak English? (formal)","r":"AH-blah oos-TEHD een-GLEHS?"},{"t":"¿Hay alguien que hable inglés?","f":"Is there someone here who speaks English?","r":"ai ahl-GYEHN keh AH-bleh een-GLEHS?"},{"t":"¡Ayuda! / ¡Socorro!","f":"Help!","r":"ah-YOO-dah!"},{"t":"Buenos días","f":"Good morning","r":"BWEH-nohs DEE-ahs"},{"t":"Buenas tardes","f":"Good afternoon / Good evening","r":"BWEH-nahs TAR-dehs"},{"t":"Buenas noches","f":"Good evening / Good night","r":"BWEH-nahs NOH-chehs"},{"t":"Señora.","f":"Mrs./Madam/Ma'am","r":"seh-NYOH-rah"},{"t":"Señor.","f":"Mr./Sir","r":"seh-NYOHR"},{"t":"Señorita.","f":"Miss","r":"seh-nyoh-REE-tah"},{"t":"Entiendo.","f":"I understand.","r":"ehn-TYEHN-doh"},{"t":"No entiendo","f":"I don't understand","r":"NOH ehn-TYEHN-doh"},{"t":"¿Podría usted hablar más despacio por favor?","f":"Could you speak more slowly please?","r":"poh-DREE-ah oos-TEHD ah-BLAHR MAHS dehs-PAH-syoh pohr fah-BO"},{"t":"¿Podría usted repetirlo por favor?","f":"Could you repeat that please?","r":"poh-DREE-ah oos-TEHD reh-peh-TEER-loh pohr fah-BOHR?"},{"t":"¿Dónde está el baño? / In Spain: ¿Dónde están los aseos?","f":"Where is the toilet?","r":"DOHN-deh ehss-TAH EHL BAH-nyoh?"}]},{"id":"es:c3","title":"@c_numbers","level":"@beginner","intro":"Counting in \"hundreds\" is unheard of for numbers larger than 1 000. This is also true for years. Instead of \"nineteen hundred fifty three\" a Spanish speaker would say \"mil novecientos cincuenta y tres\".","sections":[{"heading":"@c_remember","body":"Opposite to English, a comma \",\" is used as a decimal point when writing numbers in Spanish, while a period \".\" is used to separate groups of thousands. This means that the number written 1,000,000.5 in English will usually be written 1.000.000,5 in Spanish (though you may see 1,000,000.5 in Mexico or the U.S.)"}],"phrases":[{"t":"cero","f":"0","r":"SEH-roh"},{"t":"uno","f":"1","r":"OO-noh"},{"t":"dos","f":"2","r":"dohs"},{"t":"tres","f":"3","r":"trehs"},{"t":"cuatro","f":"4","r":"KWAH-troh"},{"t":"cinco","f":"5","r":"SEEN-koh"},{"t":"seis","f":"6","r":"SEH-ees"},{"t":"siete","f":"7","r":"see-EH-teh"},{"t":"ocho","f":"8","r":"OH-choh"},{"t":"nueve","f":"9","r":"noo-EH-beh"},{"t":"diez","f":"10","r":"dee-EHS"},{"t":"once","f":"11","r":"OHN-seh"},{"t":"doce","f":"12","r":"DOH-seh"},{"t":"trece","f":"13","r":"TREH-seh"},{"t":"catorce","f":"14","r":"kah-TOHR-seh"},{"t":"quince","f":"15","r":"KEEN-seh"},{"t":"dieciséis","f":"16","r":"dee-EH-see-SEH-ees"},{"t":"diecisiete","f":"17","r":"dee-EH-see-see-EH-teh"},{"t":"dieciocho","f":"18","r":"dee-EH-see-OH-choh"},{"t":"diecinueve","f":"19","r":"dee-EH-see-NOO-EH-beh"},{"t":"veinte","f":"20","r":"VAIN-teh"},{"t":"veintiuno","f":"21","r":"VAIN-tee-OO-noh"},{"t":"veintidós","f":"22","r":"VAIN-tee-DOHS"},{"t":"veintitrés","f":"23","r":"VAIN-tee-TREHS"},{"t":"treinta","f":"30","r":"TRAIN-tah"},{"t":"treinta y uno","f":"31","r":""},{"t":"treinta y dos","f":"32","r":""},{"t":"cuarenta","f":"40","r":"kwah-REHN-tah"},{"t":"cincuenta","f":"50","r":"seen-KWEHN-tah"},{"t":"sesenta","f":"60","r":"seh-SEHN-tah"},{"t":"setenta","f":"70","r":"seh-TEHN-tah"},{"t":"ochenta","f":"80","r":"oh-CHEHN-tah"},{"t":"noventa","f":"90","r":"noh-BEHN-tah"},{"t":"cien","f":"100","r":"see-EHN"},{"t":"ciento uno","f":"101","r":""},{"t":"ciento dos","f":"102","r":""},{"t":"doscientos","f":"200","r":"dohs-see-EHN-tohs"},{"t":"doscientos uno","f":"201","r":""},{"t":"doscientos dos","f":"202","r":""},{"t":"trescientos","f":"300","r":"trehs-see-EHN-tohs"},{"t":"quinientos","f":"500","r":"kee-nee-EHN-tohs"},{"t":"setecientos","f":"700","r":""},{"t":"novecientos","f":"900","r":""},{"t":"mil","f":"1,000","r":"MEEL"},{"t":"mil uno","f":"1,001","r":""},{"t":"mil dos","f":"1,002","r":""},{"t":"dos mil","f":"2,000","r":"dohs MEEL"},{"t":"tres mil","f":"3,000","r":""},{"t":"un millón","f":"1,000,000","r":"oon mee-YOHN"},{"t":"dos millones","f":"2,000,000","r":""},{"t":"mil millones","f":"1,000,000,000","r":"meel mee-YOH-nehs"},{"t":"un billón","f":"1,000,000,000,000","r":"oon bee-YOHN"},{"t":"número_____","f":"number _____ (train, bus, etc.)","r":"NOO-meh-roh"},{"t":"medio","f":"half","r":"MEH-dyoh"},{"t":"menos","f":"less","r":"MEH-nohs"},{"t":"más","f":"more","r":"MAHS"}]},{"id":"es:c4","title":"@c_time","level":"@beginner","intro":"When speaking, clock times are normally told using the 12-hour clock. Instead of AM/PM, a clarification can be added: de la mañana (morning), de la tarde (afternoon), de la noche (evening/night) or de la madrugada (late night). Rarely do Spanish speakers use the 24-hour system in conversation. In writing, the practice differs, see below.","sections":[{"heading":"@c_remember","body":"For numbers after one:\n; it's __ o'clock: son las __ (son lahs __)"},{"heading":"@c_remember","body":"To add the minutes, simply add the number at the end or use y (and) before the number:"},{"heading":"@c_remember","body":"Special forms:\n; quarter-past: y cuarto (ee KUAR-toh). Not to be confused with quatro (four)\n; half-past: y media (ee MEH-dyah)\n; quarter-to: menos cuarto (MEH-nos KUAR-toh)\n; noon : mediodía (meh-dyoh-DEE-ah)\n; midnight : medianoche (meh-dyah-NOH-cheh)"}],"phrases":[{"t":"ahora","f":"now","r":"ah-OH-rah"},{"t":"después","f":"later","r":"dehs-PWEHS"},{"t":"antes","f":"before","r":"AHN-tehs"},{"t":"mañana","f":"morning","r":"mah-NYAH-nah"},{"t":"tarde","f":"afternoon","r":"TAHR-deh"},{"t":"noche","f":"night","r":"NOH-cheh"},{"t":"la una","f":"one o'clock","r":"lah OOH-nah"},{"t":"es la una de la tarde","f":"it's one o'clock in the afternoon","r":""},{"t":"son las __","f":"it's __ o'clock","r":"son lahs __"},{"t":"10: las siete diez dee-EHS)","f":"07","r":""},{"t":"y cuarto. Not to be confused with quatro","f":"quarter-past","r":""},{"t":"y media","f":"half-past","r":""},{"t":"menos cuarto","f":"quarter-to","r":""},{"t":"mediodía","f":"noon","r":"meh-dyoh-DEE-ah"},{"t":"medianoche","f":"midnight","r":"meh-dyah-NOH-cheh"},{"t":"_____ minuto","f":"_____ minute(s)","r":"mee-NOO-toh(s)"},{"t":"_____ hora","f":"_____ hour(s)","r":"OH-rah(s)"},{"t":"_____ día","f":"_____ day(s)","r":"DEE-ah(s)"},{"t":"_____ semana","f":"_____ week(s)","r":"seh-MAH-nah(s)"},{"t":"_____ mes","f":"_____ month(s)","r":"MEHS-(ehs)"},{"t":"_____ año","f":"_____ year(s)","r":"AH-nyoh(s)"},{"t":"hoy","f":"today","r":"oy"},{"t":"ayer","f":"yesterday","r":"ah-YEHR"},{"t":"esta semana","f":"this week","r":"EHS-tah seh-MAH-nah"},{"t":"la semana pasada","f":"last week","r":"lah seh-MAH-nah pah-SAH-dah"},{"t":"la semana que viene","f":"next week","r":"lah seh-MAH-nah keh BYEH-neh"},{"t":"domingo","f":"Sunday","r":"doh-MEEN-goh"},{"t":"lunes","f":"Monday","r":"LOO-nehs"},{"t":"martes","f":"Tuesday","r":"MAHR-tehs"},{"t":"miércoles","f":"Wednesday","r":"MYEHR-koh-lehs"},{"t":"jueves","f":"Thursday","r":"HWEH-vehs"},{"t":"viernes","f":"Friday","r":"VYEHR-nehs"},{"t":"sábado","f":"Saturday","r":"SAH-bah-doh"},{"t":"enero","f":"January","r":"eh-NEH-roh"},{"t":"febrero","f":"February","r":"feh-BREH-roh"},{"t":"marzo","f":"March","r":"MAR-soh"},{"t":"abril","f":"April","r":"ah-BREEL"},{"t":"mayo","f":"May","r":"MAH-yoh"},{"t":"junio","f":"June","r":"HOO-nyoh"},{"t":"julio","f":"July","r":"HOO-lyoh"},{"t":"agosto","f":"August","r":"ah-GOHS-toh"},{"t":"septiembre","f":"September","r":"sehp-TYEHM-breh"},{"t":"octubre","f":"October","r":"ohk-TOO-breh"},{"t":"noviembre","f":"November","r":"noh-VYEHM-breh"},{"t":"diciembre","f":"December","r":"dee-SYEHM-breh"},{"t":"nueve de la mañana, 9:00, 9:00 AM","f":"9 o'clock AM","r":""},{"t":"30 PM:doce y media de la mañana, 12:30, 12:30 PM","f":"12","r":""},{"t":"una de la tarde, 13:00, 1:00 PM","f":"1 o'clock PM","r":""},{"t":"diez de la noche, 22:00, 10:00 PM","f":"10 o'clock PM","r":""},{"t":"dos de la madrugada or dos de la mañana, 2:00, 2:00 AM","f":"2 o'clock AM","r":""},{"t":"7 de mayo de 2003","f":"7 May 2003","r":""},{"t":"23 de octubre de 1997","f":"23 October 1997","r":""},{"t":"23 de febrero, date of a failed coup d'état in Spain","f":"23-F","r":""},{"t":"11 de septiembre, date of the attack to the Twin Towers.","f":"11-S","r":""}]},{"id":"es:c5","title":"@c_colours","level":"@beginner","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"negro","f":"black","r":"NEH-groh"},{"t":"blanco","f":"white","r":"BLAHN-koh"},{"t":"gris","f":"gray","r":"GREES"},{"t":"rojo","f":"red","r":"ROH-hoh"},{"t":"azul","f":"blue","r":"ah-SOOL"},{"t":"amarillo","f":"yellow","r":"ah-mah-REE-yoh"},{"t":"verde","f":"green","r":"BEHR-deh"},{"t":"naranja , anaranjado","f":"orange","r":"nah-RAHN-hah"},{"t":"púrpura , morado, violeta","f":"purple","r":"POOR-poo-rah"},{"t":"rosa","f":"pink","r":"ROH-sah"},{"t":"marrón, café, castaño.","f":"brown","r":"mahr-ROHN"}]},{"id":"es:c6","title":"@c_problems","level":"@elementary","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"Déjame en paz.","f":"Leave me alone.","r":"DEH-hah-meh ehn PAHS"},{"t":"¡No me toques!","f":"Don't touch me!","r":"noh meh TOH-kehs!"},{"t":"Llamaré a la policía.","f":"I'll call the police.","r":"yah-mah-REH ah lah poh-lee-SEE-ah"},{"t":"¡Policía!","f":"Police!","r":"poh-lee-SEE-ah!"},{"t":"¡Alto, ladrón!","f":"Stop! Thief!","r":"AHL-toh, lah-DROHN!"},{"t":"Necesito ayuda.","f":"I need help.","r":"neh-seh-SEE-toh ah-YOO-dah"},{"t":"Es una emergencia.","f":"It's an emergency.","r":"ehs OO-nah eh-mehr-HEHN-syah"},{"t":"Estoy perdido/a","f":"I'm lost.","r":"ehs-TOY pehr-DEE-doh/dah"},{"t":"Perdí mi bolsa/bolso/cartera.","f":"I lost my purse/handbag.","r":"pehr-DEE mee BOHL-sah / BOHL-soh / kahr-TEH-rah"},{"t":"Perdí la cartera/billetera.","f":"I lost my wallet.","r":"pehr-DEE lah kahr-TEH-rah / bee-yeh-TEH-rah"},{"t":"Estoy enfermo/a.","f":"I'm sick.","r":"ehs-TOY ehn-FEHR-moh/mah"},{"t":"Estoy herido/a.","f":"I've been injured.","r":"ehs-TOY heh-REE-doh/dah"},{"t":"Necesito un médico.","f":"I need a doctor.","r":"neh-seh-SEE-toh OON MEH-dee-coh"},{"t":"¿Puedo usar su teléfono?","f":"Can I use your phone?","r":"PWEH-doh oo-SAHR soo teh-LEH-foh-noh?"},{"t":"¿Me presta su celular/móvil?","f":"Can I borrow your cell phone/mobile phone?","r":"meh PREHS-tah soo seh-loo-LAHR / MOH-beel?"},{"t":"Necesito llamar a la embajada","f":"I need to call the embassy.","r":"neh-seh-SEE-toh yah-MAHR ah lah em-bah-HAH-dah"}]},{"id":"es:c7","title":"@c_transport","level":"@elementary","intro":"The verb \"to take\", as in to take a bus or train is coger in Spain, and tomar in Latin America. The former is not used in this context in Latin America, where it is a vulgar sexual term.","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"¡Taxi!","f":"Taxi!","r":"TAHK-see"},{"t":"Lléveme a _____, por favor.","f":"Take me to _____, please.","r":"YEH-beh-meh ah"},{"t":"¿Cuanto cuesta ir hasta/a _____?","f":"How much does it cost to get to _____?","r":"KWA